  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs leasing a residential unit at ₱15,000 per month subject to VAT?\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, leasing a residential unit at ₱15,000 per month is subject to VAT, as it falls under the definition of the lease of properties for consideration.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provisions are found in the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), specifically:\n- **Section 106**: Value-Added Tax on Sale of Goods or Properties\n- **Section 108**: Value-Added Tax on the Sale of Services, Including the Use or Lease of Properties\n\n### Legal Interpretation\nUnder **Section 106** of the NIRC, there is a value-added tax levied on every sale, barter, or exchange of goods or properties, which includes the lease of properties. **Section 108** further specifies that VAT is applicable to the lease of properties, indicating that the gross sales derived from such leases are subject to a 12% VAT. Since the lease amount of ₱15,000 exceeds the threshold for VAT exemption, the transaction is taxable.\n\n### Practical Application\nIn practice, landlords leasing residential units for ₱15,000 per month must register for VAT if they are not already registered and charge the appropriate VAT on the lease payments. They are also required to issue VAT invoices to tenants and remit the collected VAT to the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR). This ensures compliance with tax obligations and avoids potential penalties for non-compliance.",
